 | `pl::markup` | the job | `overhead` and `profit`. Neither has a default. | `%` |
 | `pl::level` | a Storey | `elevation`: how high its floor is above the job's datum. `height`: how far up to the next floor. `computation_height`, when it is not the usual 4 ft: how far above its floor its rooms find the walls that bound them. | each `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
 | `pl::wall_type` | a Type | `thickness`. `layers`, when it is built up: each a `function` (structure, substrate, thermal, membrane, finish), a `material` and a `thickness`, from the exterior in. They add up to the thickness. `footing: true` where the type was made for strip footings (its thickness is their width, and only footings are drawn on it; call 87); left out for a wall's type. | each `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
+| `pl::joists_span` | a Floor (call 165) | Which way its joists run: `the short way` (square to the floor's longest edge) or `the long way`, with how sure that is and where it came from. Written to a file only where one is said; not said, the short way is taken, Assumed, and every joist figure rests on it. Figures, only where a priced line names the joists ("Floor joists, 2x10 at 16 in": a lumber size and a spacing, by `finish::members_named`): `joists` (EA), `joist_length` (LF), `joist_longest` (ft), `rim_length` (LF). A roof plane, where a line of it or of its roof names the rafters ("Rafters, 2x8 at 24 in"), has `rafters` (EA), `rafter_length` (LF, on the slope) and `rafter_longest` (ft), and the roof the sums. The layout is `building::members::members_across`: a member 1 1/2 in thick, an edge member 3/4 in inside each edge that runs with the members, marks at each multiple of the spacing from the first side, a piece under 6 in left out and said; blocking, hangers and doubled members are not counted. |
 | `pl::floor_type` | a Type | `thickness`, and `layers` from the top down, as a wall type's. | each `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
 | `pl::opening_type` | a Type | `category`: Door or Window. `width`, `height`. | each `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
-| `pl::segment_type` | a Type | `category`: Duct, Pipe, Conduit or Cable. `round` and `width` (the diameter), or `width` and `depth`. | each `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
+| `pl::segment_type` | a Type | `category`: Duct, Pipe, Conduit, Cable, or (call 164) Beam, Post or PadFooting. `round` and `width` (the diameter), or `width` and `depth`. `weight_per_foot`, a beam type's only and left out where there is none: what a foot of it weighs, typed (`18 lb/ft`), with how sure it is and where it came from; nothing sizes a beam. | each `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
 | `pl::wall` | a Wall, under a Storey | `type`: the path of a Type with a `pl::wall_type`. `line`: its location line on the plan, first end to second; its exterior side is to the left of that. `location`, when the line is not its center line: `core-center`, `exterior-finish`, `interior-finish`, `exterior-core`, `interior-core`. `top_level` (the path of the Storey its top is held to) with `top_offset` if any, or `height`: one or the other. `base_offset`, when it does not stand on its level's floor. `room_bounding: false` and `join: false` when it does not bound rooms or join its neighbours. | `line`: `ft` or `in`. The others: `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
 | `pl::opening` | a Door or Window, under its Wall | `type`: the path of a Type with a `pl::opening_type` of its kind. `offset`: how far its **center** is along the wall's line from the line's first end. `sill`: how high its bottom is above the wall's base. | each `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
 | `pl::floor` | a Floor, under a Storey | `type`: the path of a Type with a `pl::floor_type`. `boundary` on the plan. `offset`, when its top is not at its level's floor level. | `boundary`: `ft` or `in` |
 | `pl::room_line` | a Room line, under a Storey (call 152) | `line`: its two ends on the plan. It bounds the rooms of its level as a wall of no thickness would, and is not built: no figures, no cost line, no assembly, no allowance, no work mark; never counted as a wall; not clash-tested; not written to the BIM file. A room measured to it is no surer than it. | `line`: `ft` or `in` |
 | `pl::room` | a Room, under a Storey | `point`: a point inside it. `number`: what the plan calls it. Its boundary is not stored: it is found from the walls. | `point`: `ft` or `in` |
 | `pl::segment` | a Duct, Pipe, Conduit or Cable, under a Storey | One straight piece. `type`: the path of a Type with a `pl::segment_type` of its kind. `line`: its two ends on the plan. `offset`: how high it is above its level's floor at the first end; `end_offset` at the second, when it is not level. `justify`, when its heights are those of its `top` or `bottom` and not its center. | `line`: `ft` or `in`. The others: `ft`, `in` or `ft-in` |
+| `pl::segment` (beam) | a Beam, under a Storey (call 164) | Held as a run by `pl::segment`: its two ends on the plan, `offset` the height of its **top** above its level's floor (its `justify` is `top`), a type of the category Beam. Refused: too short; its ends one over the other (a beam runs along, and what stands is a post); sloped more than 75 degrees. Figures: `length` (LF), `width`, `depth` (ft), `weight` (LB, only where its type gives a weight per foot), `section_area` (SF) and `volume` (CY) for the BIM file. It joins no fitting and is on no system. |
+| `pl::column` | a Post or a Pad footing, under a Storey (call 164) | BIMCraft's `Column`: `type` (a `pl::segment_type` of the category Post or PadFooting), `at` on the plan, `base_offset` above its level's floor (below it, negative), `height` (a pad footing: its thickness). A pad footing is a post laid flat: its top is its base and its thickness. Written to a file only where a job has one. Figures: a post's `height` (LF), `width`, `depth`, `section_area`, `volume`; a pad footing's `width`, `length`, `thickness`, `plan_area` (SF), `form_area` (SF, 2 x (width + length) x thickness) and `volume` (CY). |
 | `pl::placed` | Equipment, a Fixture or a Fitting, under a Storey | `what` it is. `at`: the middle of its base on the plan. `offset`: how high that is above its level's floor. `angle`, when it is turned: degrees counter-clockwise from east. `size`: along its own x, along its own y, tall. | `at`, `size`: `ft` or `in`. `offset`: `ft`, `in` or `ft-in`. `angle`: `deg` |
 | `pl::fitting` | a Fitting | `kind`: bend, tee, cross, transition or coupling. | |
 | `pl::connectors` | Equipment, a Fixture or a Fitting | A list. Each: `name`; `kind` (pipe, duct, conduit, power); for a pipe, `domain` (cold, hot, waste, vent, storm, gas); `flow` (in, out) when it is not both; `at`, in the thing's own axes (x along its angle, y to its left, z up from its base); `size`. | `at`: `ft` or `in` |
